<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Liberation Front of Angola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as FNLA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-algeria">National Liberation Front</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> (FNLA) is an Angolan nationalist movement led for decades by Holden Roberto, with its base among the Bakongo of the north. Formed from earlier Bakongo movements and renamed in 1962, it fought Portuguese rule from bases in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> after the rising of 1961. After independence in 1975 it lost the struggle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a> and declined into a minor party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-of-angola.jpg" alt="National Liberation Front of Angola" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dahn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FNLA_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
